  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThrough Tonight\n\nA Canadian cold front will move southeast through the area this\nafternoon and evening. With cyclonic flow and cold air \nadvection aloft, the CAMs are indicating isolated to scattered \nshowers mainly located north of Interstate 90. At this time, \nkept the 20 percent chance of showers across north-central \nWisconsin. High temperatures will range from the lower 70s to \nlower 80s.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-40s to mid-50s tonight.\n\nWednesday through Friday night\n\nHigh pressure will provide mainly dry weather from Wednesday\nthrough Friday night. Even though there is northwest flow \naloft, the fires along the US/Canadian border are far smaller \nthan those in Canada's boreal forest, so the HRRR is continuing \nto show very low concentrations of surface smoke and the \nconcentrations of smoke at 6K feet also remain generally light \ntoo.\n\nHigh temperatures will gradually warm through Friday. High\ntemperatures will be in the lower and mid-70s on Wednesday, and\nrange from the mid-70s to lower 80s on Thursday and Friday. \n\nLow temperatures will mainly be in the 50s and lower 60s.\n\nWeekend\n\nA warm front will move east and northeast through the region on\nSaturday night and Sunday morning. A mesoscale convective \ncomplex will likely develop over the Northern Plains and then \ntrack into the Upper Mississippi River Valley during the \novernight and Sunday morning. Due to the uncertainty on its \ntrack, kept rain chances in the 20 to 50 percent range. With the\n3-6 km shear less than 30 knots, severe weather chances for \norganized convection remains on the low side.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the lower to mid-80s on Saturday\nand ranges from the mid-80s to lower 90s.",
